<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walt Whitman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Walter Whitman Jr.</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walt Whitman was an American poet, essayist and journalist, often called the father of free verse. Born in West Hills, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, on Long Island, he left formal schooling at 11 and worked as a printer, journalist, teacher and government clerk. In 1855 he published Leaves of Grass at his own expense; some called its open sensuality obscene, and he kept expanding and revising it until his death. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> he cared for the wounded in the hospitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assassination-of-abraham-lincoln">assassination of Abraham Lincoln</a> he wrote poems including &quot;O Captain! My Captain!&quot;. After a stroke in 1873 he moved to Camden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, where he died in 1892.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walt-whitman.jpg" alt="Walt Whitman" width="330" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Collins Cox / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walt_Whitman_-_George_Collins_Cox.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I believe a leaf of grass is no less than the journey work of the stars, And the pismire is equally perfect, and a grain of sand, and the egg of the wren…&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;Song of Myself&quot;, section 31, Leaves of Grass (1855; final text 1892) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1322/pg1322-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I celebrate myself, and sing myself, And what I assume you shall assume, For every atom belonging to me as good belongs to you.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;Song of Myself&quot;, section 1, Leaves of Grass (1855; final text 1892) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1322/pg1322-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
